{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>כי מי</b>. This too returns to the matter of the poor man who knows: since a person does not know what is good for him in life — whether wealth or poverty — and the days of his fleeting life are numbered, it is fitting that he make them like a passing shadow and not seek the departure of the soul until he leaves his money, for nothing he leaves will avail — nor does he know what will be done with his money after him." "And the sense of <b>ויעשם כצל</b> (he makes them like a shadow): that he should consider in his thought — just as a shadow does not stand for a single moment, so his days will pass — they are the essence, even though all that befalls them, from good to evil, wealth and poverty. And there is another interpretation on the verse, and the fool multiplies words."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:27:59.740421768Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}